// THE FOUR-PHASE DRed EPOCH DRIVER — A RECURSIVE `rel` MAINTAINED ACROSS EPOCHS
// UNDER INSERTS *AND* RETRACTIONS, THROUGH ONE EMITTED FN.
//
// Until this slice the four phases existed as four fns and NOTHING CALLED THEM
// IN ORDER. `pass/wql_incr_rel_od_phase1.logos` and `..._phase3.logos` perform
// the sequence themselves, in the fixture, which proves the phases and proves
// nothing about a caller: the argument shapes, the capture point of `__b0`, the
// direction `__edb` moves, the sign of the group fold and the error contract are
// all decisions the FIXTURE was making. `__wql_<q>_scc<c>_dred` is the emitted
// party that makes them, and this file is where it is exercised.
//
//     __wql_tc_scc0_dred(&mut h, de)     ← DE in the SLICE parameter, exactly
//                                          where `tc_epoch` takes the inserts
//
// ⚠⚠ THIS IS NOT THE RETRACTION SURFACE. `incr_retract_eligible` still refuses
// every rel-backed query with recursion as its ground; `tc_retract`,
// `tc_apply` and `__wd` still do not exist, and the three `fail/` doors that
// assert those names absent (`wql_incr_rel_no_retract`,
// `wql_incr_mutrec_no_retract_{odd,even}`) are UNTOUCHED and still true. The
// driver is a private item reachable only by the mangled name this file uses —
// the same footing phases 1 and 2 landed on. The surface round re-parents this
// body into a rel sub-arm of the `retr` branch and replaces those doors then.
//
// ══ WHAT IS ASSERTED, AND AGAINST WHAT ═══════════════════════════════════════
// At EVERY epoch, insert and retract alike, against oracles that share no code
// with the emitter:
//
//   • `tc_snapshot(&h)` == the group table of `naive_closure(I ∖ R)` — as a SET
//     with equal length, so a duplicated group is a disagreement.
//   • `h.__tot_path`    == `naive_closure(I ∖ R)` — the RELATION, not only the
//     answer, and with equal length so a duplicate row cannot hide.
//   • `h.__edb`         == `I ∖ R` — ⚠ THE ONE ASSERTION THAT CATCHES A
//     RETRACTION THAT UNDOES ITSELF. If the driver left the accumulated input
//     intact, the NEXT insert epoch's round-0 pass over it re-derives every
//     retracted fact and the relation silently heals one epoch later. Every
//     single-epoch oracle in this arc is blind to that, which is why the cells
//     below always INSERT AFTER RETRACTING.
//   • `h.__lat`         == (|OD|, |S|, |RD|) — the INTERMEDIATE sets, against an
//     independent OD oracle AND a hand-computed triple per cell. An answer
//     oracle is blind to this arc (measured twice in `emit_scc_od_fns`: delete
//     phase 1a's existing-rel⋈DE seed term and the diamond's final answer is
//     byte-for-byte correct while OD, S and RD all move), so without the latch a
//     retraction that recomputed the whole closure from scratch would pass every
//     other check in this file.
//   • `h.__lat[2] != -1` — the sentinel `_cpt` pushes because |RD| is not a fact
//     in its scope. The driver is the party that retires it; a `-1` surviving a
//     successful call means phase 3's accounting did not run.
//
// ── THE OD ORACLE, AND WHY IT IS NOT "THE ANSWER GOT SMALLER" ───────────────
// `OD = { f ∈ T : SOME derivation of f uses a row of DE }`, which for transitive
// closure is exactly the pairs joined by a walk through at least one deleted
// edge. `od_oracle` computes that DIRECTLY — for each `(x,y) ∈ closure(ALL)`, is
// there a `(u,v) ∈ DE` with `x ⇝* u` and `v ⇝* y` (reflexive) — from a
// from-scratch closure that has never heard of a phase, a shadow set or a delta.
// It is a STRICT superset of `closure(ALL) ∖ closure(PRES)` whenever a fact has
// a second surviving derivation, which is exactly what makes phase 3
// load-bearing rather than decorative, and cell 1 (the DIAMOND) is where that
// strictness is visible.
//
// ══ THE CELLS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  1 DIAMOND         1→2, 1→3, 2→4, 3→4; retract (2,4). `(1,4)` is
//                    OVER-DELETED and RE-DERIVED — |OD| > |OD ∖ RD| — so a
//                    driver that stopped after phase 2 answers a relation
//                    missing a true fact, and one that never ran phase 1's
//                    existing-rel⋈DE seed term answers correctly with the wrong
//                    intermediate sets.
//  2 MID-CHAIN       1→2→3→4→5; retract (3,4). `RD = ∅`: phase 2 alone is
//                    already the right answer, so every "re-derive on principle"
//                    defect shows up here as a non-empty RD while the ANSWER
//                    stays correct.
//  3 SELF-SUPPORTING 1→2→3→1; retract (3,1). Every one of the nine pairs has a
//    CYCLE           walk through the deleted edge, so `S = ∅` and phase 3 is
//                    the ENTIRE answer. The cell where "the total is a lower
//                    bound of the fixpoint" is the empty set.
//
// Each cell then INSERTS again on the survivor handle and re-checks everything,
// which is the `__edb` shrinkage witness described above.
//
// ══ THE PRECONDITION: `DE ⊆ EDB` ═════════════════════════════════════════════
// Block D. Retracting a row never inserted would seed OD with a fact the total
// never held and break `OD ⊆ T`. The driver refuses it with
// `ElError::RetractAbsent` before phase 1a runs.
// ⚠ THAT IS A CHEAP REFUSAL AND THIS FILE SAYS SO. It fires before anything has
// moved, so "the handle is untouched afterwards" is a claim about a handle
// nothing touched. It is asserted anyway — the row-footprint precedent
// (`8f9a8059`) closed a SHIPPING silent wrong answer on the non-rel surface, and
// the check that is present is the one that has to be pinned — but the ERROR
// CONTRACT is pinned by block E, not by this one.
// ⚠ AND THE REL PATH ESCAPES BOTH DEGENERACIES OF THAT PRECEDENT, which is why
// block D also retracts a row that differs from an inserted one in ONE FIELD the
// fold never reads: `__edb` stores exact input rows, not a projection of the
// fold's read fields, so membership is identity. On the non-rel surface those
// two cases are annotated-green defects; here they are simply refused.
//
// ══ THE ERROR CONTRACT, PINNED BY A **LATE** FAULT ═══════════════════════════
// Block E. THE CONTRACT: **`<q>_dred` either returns `Ok` and the handle has
// advanced by exactly one retraction, or it returns `Err` and the handle is
// BYTE-IDENTICAL to its pre-call state — content and order, in every field.**
//
// A guard that fires before anything happened proves nothing, so the fault is
// placed as late as it can be placed: `sq` sums an i64 column, the driver's
// NEGATIVE fold computes `el_mul(-1, arg)`, and `el_mul(-1, i64::MIN)`
// OVERFLOWS. By the time it does, `_od` has scanned the whole previous total,
// `_odp` has run to fixpoint, `_cpt` has counted and compacted, `_i` has run its
// naïve round 0 and its delta rounds, the latch has been written and the
// survivor input and every member total have been committed — TO THE DRIVER'S
// PRIVATE COPY. The block asserts, after the `Err`, that the caller's `__edb`,
// `__tot_spath`, `__lat` and `sq_snapshot` are all exactly what they were.
// ⚠ WHAT WOULD FAIL IT: the insert path's own containment. `<q>_epoch` builds
// the relation in locals and commits after the driver returns, but its GROUP
// FOLD writes `__h` directly and its header states the window that leaves. Doing
// the same here would leave `__edb` shrunk, the total compacted and re-derived,
// the latch rewritten and ONE group's cells folded down — a residual
// incomparable with any specification, which is precisely the interpreter's
// measured anti-example (`stdlib/mem/deem/incr_rec.logos`, `fn dred`: a residual
// incomparable in both directions, collateral loss in a different connected
// component, `is_converged()` true in every faulted state, and an ABSORBING
// faulted state that re-faults on the very retraction that would clear it).
// ⚠ WHY `_cpt`'s OWN REFUSAL IS **NOT** THE LATE FAULT HERE. `_cpt` refuses
// `OD ⊄ T` after phases 1a and 1b have run — genuinely late — but that input is
// UNREACHABLE THROUGH THE DRIVER, because `DE ⊆ EDB` makes `OD ⊆ T` a theorem.
// Reaching it would mean bypassing the check that makes the driver correct, so
// the late fault is taken on a route that survives every check: arithmetic.
//
// ══ WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT SAY ══════════════════════════════════════════════
// ⚠ NOT A WORD ABOUT COST, AND NO CLOCK ANYWHERE. DRed is a measured
// PESSIMIZATION (1.15–1.9× a from-scratch recompute, losing worse as |RD|
// grows, no crossover in the reachable range); the number is recorded in
// `emit_scc_od_fns`'s header and is NOT re-measured — the box is shared, and a
// ratio from a shared box looks like evidence and is not.
// ⚠ EVERY CELL IS N = 1 (a single-member SCC). The N-member driver path is
// emitted from the same loops and is exercised by
// `wql_incr_rel_mutrec_epochs` on the INSERT side only; a mutually recursive
// retraction witness is open work, and `fail/wql_incr_mutrec_no_retract_even`
// still names the shape where a first-member-instead-of-selected-member bug
// hides.
//
// ══ CONTROLS, MEASURED (archives dropped, whole stdlib rebuilt for each) ═════
// Every code below was PREDICTED before the run and is the code that came back.
//
//  • `__h.__edb = __pres;` DELETED from the driver — the accumulated input does
//    not shrink, so the retraction survives exactly one epoch.
//    → **exit 44** — cell 1, `check` code 4, at the FIRST retraction. The
//    ANSWER, the RELATION and both other checks were all still correct at that
//    point; only the input assertion spoke, which is what it is there for.
//  • THE −1 FOLD LOOP DELETED — only the post-`_cpt` suffix is folded.
//    → **exit 42** — cell 1, `check` code 2. The diamond's group 2 keeps its
//    count of 1 after its only row leaves (no tombstone) and group 1 reads 4
//    where 3 is right. The RELATION is untouched by this defect, so `check`'s
//    code 3 stays silent: only the ANSWER speaks, and it is the aggregate-side
//    staleness `_cpt`'s `__rm_<m>` note predicts.
//  • THE `|RD|` SENTINEL-RETIRING LOOP DISABLED.
//    → **exit 57** — cell 1, code 17: `__lat[2]` comes back `-1`.
//  • THE `DE ⊆ __edb` REFUSAL WEAKENED TO A NO-OP.
//    → **exit 207** — and ⚠⚠ ONLY `bad4` SPOKE. `(9,9)`, `(1,9)` and the mixed
//    batch were all STILL refused, one phase later, by `_cpt`'s `OD ⊄ T` pass.
//    So three of the four rows in block D are not evidence about this check at
//    all; the fourth — retracting a pair that is DERIVED but was never an INPUT
//    — is the only one `_cpt` cannot see, and it is why the check exists.
//    Measured, not reasoned: the prediction named exit 207 and the reason the
//    other three stayed green was found by running them.
//  • `__hh.__edb` COMMITTED BEFORE THE FOLDS (atomicity broken for one field).
//    → **exit 214** — block E: after the late fault the caller's `__edb` is the
//    survivor set while every other field is pre-call. The residual is
//    INCOMPARABLE with either state, which is the interpreter's measured defect
//    reproduced in one field.
//    ⚠ AND THE STRONGER FORM OF THAT EDIT DOES NOT COMPILE. Moving the whole
//    commit above the two fold loops is rejected by the borrow checker —
//    `use of moved field '__h.__gk' (moved on line 2)` — because the commit
//    MOVES each Vec out of the shadow handle and `core` reads them afterwards.
//    The ordering this contract needs is therefore enforced by the language on
//    the general shape and by this fixture on the copy-instead-of-move dodge.
//  • THE DRIVER NOT EMITTED AT ALL (`dred` forced false).
//    → this file fails to compile (`call to undefined function
//    '__wql_tc_scc0_dred'`) AND `logos_09_incr_mutrec_refusal` (tier_commit)
//    reds with two violations on its `rel dred driver` axis — the axis that
//    reads the verdict for a MUTUALLY RECURSIVE SCC, which no fixture here can
//    see because every cell in this file is N = 1.
//
// exit 0 = every cell agrees with the from-scratch fixpoint over `I ∖ R` at
// every epoch, on the answer, the relation, the accumulated input and all three
// latches; `DE ⊆ EDB` refuses two shapes the non-rel footprint sensor cannot;
// and a fault after all four phases leaves the handle byte-identical.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.std.wql.el;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct E { pub a: i64, pub b: i64 }

pub deem tc(es: &[E]) {
    rel path(a: i64, b: i64) {
        from es e select (e.a, e.b);
        from path p join es e on p.b == e.a select (p.a, e.b);
    }
    from path p group by p.a aggregate c = count() select (key, c)
}

// A SECOND query over its own rel, differing in ONE thing: the aggregate is an
// integer `sum` rather than a `count`. That is what makes block E's late fault
// reachable — `count`'s negative fold is `el_add(gc, -1)` and cannot overflow,
// while `sum`'s is `el_add(acc, el_mul(-1, arg))` and `el_mul(-1, i64::MIN)`
// does. It is ALSO a second admission witness: `sum` over a non-float
// accumulator is what `retract_ok` admits, so the driver's D4 clause is exercised
// on its true side here and on its false side by the absence of any driver for a
// float `sum` (there is no `__wql_*_dred` to name for one).
pub deem sq(es: &[E]) {
    rel spath(a: i64, b: i64) {
        from es e select (e.a, e.b);
        from spath p join es e on p.b == e.a select (p.a, e.b);
    }
    from spath p group by p.a aggregate s = sum(p.b) select (key, s)
}

// ══ THE ORACLES — no delta, no shadow set, no SCC, no phase ═════════════════
fn pair_uniq(p: &mut Vec<(i64, i64)>, a: i64, b: i64) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < p.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = p.get(i);
        if c.0 == a && c.1 == b { return false; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    p.push((a, b));
    return true;
}

fn pair_has(p: &Vec<(i64, i64)>, a: i64, b: i64) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < p.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = p.get(i);
        if c.0 == a && c.1 == b { return true; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return false;
}

fn naive_closure(es: &[E]) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let mut p: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < es.len() {
        pair_uniq(&mut p, es[i].a, es[i].b);
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    let mut grew: bool = true;
    while grew {
        grew = false;
        let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
        while k < p.len() {
            let pr: (i64, i64) = p.get(k);
            let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
            while j < es.len() {
                if pr.1 == es[j].a {
                    if pair_uniq(&mut p, pr.0, es[j].b) { grew = true; }
                }
                j = j + 1i64;
            }
            k = k + 1i64;
        }
    }
    return p;
}

// Set equality: mutual containment PLUS equal length, so a DUPLICATE on either
// side is a disagreement rather than something containment absorbs.
fn same_set(x: &Vec<(i64, i64)>, y: &Vec<(i64, i64)>) -> bool {
    if x.len() != y.len() { return false; }
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < x.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = x.get(i);
        if !pair_has(y, c.0, c.1) { return false; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
    while j < y.len() {
        let c2: (i64, i64) = y.get(j);
        if !pair_has(x, c2.0, c2.1) { return false; }
        j = j + 1i64;
    }
    return true;
}

// The GROUP TABLE of a relation: one row per distinct first component carrying
// its multiplicity. Written here rather than taken from `tc()` so that the
// answer check does not compare the emitter against itself.
fn groups_of(cl: &Vec<(i64, i64)>) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let mut ks: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut cs: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < cl.len() {
        let p: (i64, i64) = cl.get(i);
        let mut f: i64 = -1i64;
        let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
        while j < ks.len() { if ks.get(j) == p.0 { f = j; } j = j + 1i64; }
        if f < 0i64 { ks.push(p.0); cs.push(1i64); }
        else { cs.set(f, cs.get(f) + 1i64); }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    let mut o: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
    while k < ks.len() { o.push((ks.get(k), cs.get(k))); k = k + 1i64; }
    return o;
}

// The same table for `sum(p.b)`.
fn sums_of(cl: &Vec<(i64, i64)>) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let mut ks: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut cs: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < cl.len() {
        let p: (i64, i64) = cl.get(i);
        let mut f: i64 = -1i64;
        let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
        while j < ks.len() { if ks.get(j) == p.0 { f = j; } j = j + 1i64; }
        if f < 0i64 { ks.push(p.0); cs.push(p.1); }
        else { cs.set(f, cs.get(f) + p.1); }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    let mut o: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
    while k < ks.len() { o.push((ks.get(k), cs.get(k))); k = k + 1i64; }
    return o;
}

fn ed(a: i64, b: i64) -> E { return E { a: a, b: b }; }

fn edge_has(v: &[E], a: i64, b: i64) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < v.len() {
        let e: E = v[i];
        if e.a == a && e.b == b { return true; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return false;
}

fn survivors(all: &[E], de: &[E]) -> Vec<E> {
    let mut out: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < all.len() {
        let e: E = all[i];
        if !edge_has(de, e.a, e.b) { out.push(e); }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return out;
}

// Reflexive reachability over a from-scratch closure — `x ⇝* u`.
fn reach(cl: &Vec<(i64, i64)>, x: i64, u: i64) -> bool {
    if x == u { return true; }
    return pair_has(cl, x, u);
}

// ── THE OD ORACLE. `OD = { f ∈ T : SOME derivation of f uses a row of DE }`,
// decomposed at the deleted edge: `(x,y) ∈ OD` iff some `(u,v) ∈ DE` has
// `x ⇝* u` and `v ⇝* y` in the FULL graph. No phase, no shadow set, no delta.
fn od_oracle(all: &[E], de: &[E]) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let cl: Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_closure(all);
    let mut o: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < cl.len() {
        let p: (i64, i64) = cl.get(i);
        let mut hit: bool = false;
        let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
        while j < de.len() {
            let d: E = de[j];
            if reach(&cl, p.0, d.a) && reach(&cl, d.b, p.1) { hit = true; }
            j = j + 1i64;
        }
        if hit { o.push((p.0, p.1)); }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return o;
}

// The handle's accumulated input, as a Vec the oracles can be asked about.
fn edb_copy(h: &TcIncr) -> Vec<E> {
    let mut o: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < h.__edb.len() { let e: E = h.__edb.get(i); o.push(ed(e.a, e.b)); i = i + 1i64; }
    return o;
}

fn tot_copy(h: &TcIncr) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let mut o: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < h.__tot_path.len() { let p: (i64, i64) = h.__tot_path.get(i); o.push((p.0, p.1)); i = i + 1i64; }
    return o;
}

fn edges_same(x: &[E], y: &[E]) -> bool {
    if x.len() != y.len() { return false; }
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < x.len() { let e: E = x[i]; if !edge_has(y, e.a, e.b) { return false; } i = i + 1i64; }
    let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
    while j < y.len() { let e2: E = y[j]; if !edge_has(x, e2.a, e2.b) { return false; } j = j + 1i64; }
    return true;
}

// ══ ONE CHECK OF EVERYTHING, at whatever epoch it is asked ═════════════════
// `cur` is the caller's independent record of `I ∖ R`. Codes 1..9.
fn check(h: &TcIncr, cur: &[E]) -> i32 {
    let cl: Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_closure(cur);
    // the ANSWER
    let snap: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match tc_snapshot(h) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; }
    };
    let want: Vec<(i64, i64)> = groups_of(&cl);
    if !same_set(&snap, &want) { return 2i32; }
    // the RELATION
    let tot: Vec<(i64, i64)> = tot_copy(h);
    if !same_set(&tot, &cl) { return 3i32; }
    // the ACCUMULATED INPUT — the retraction-that-undoes-itself sensor
    let acc: Vec<E> = edb_copy(h);
    if !edges_same(acc.as_slice(), cur) { return 4i32; }
    return 0i32;
}

// ── ONE RETRACTION EPOCH, WITH ITS LATCHES CHECKED TWICE ──────────────────
// against `od_oracle` (independent) AND against a hand-computed triple. Codes
// 1..9 from `check`, 10..19 here.
fn retract_epoch(h: &mut TcIncr, cur: &mut Vec<E>, de: &[E],
                 w_od: i64, w_s: i64, w_rd: i64) -> i32 {
    // The oracles are computed from the PRE-call state, because `OD` and `S` are
    // properties of the total the driver is about to rewrite.
    let od: Vec<(i64, i64)> = od_oracle(cur.as_slice(), de);
    let t_all: Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_closure(cur.as_slice());
    let pres: Vec<E> = survivors(cur.as_slice(), de);
    let cl_pres: Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_closure(pres.as_slice());
    let o_od: i64 = od.len();
    let o_s:  i64 = t_all.len() - od.len();
    let o_rd: i64 = cl_pres.len() - o_s;
    // ⚠ THE HAND-COMPUTED TRIPLE IS CHECKED AGAINST THE ORACLE BEFORE THE
    // ENGINE IS ASKED ANYTHING. Two numbers that were both derived from the same
    // run of the same oracle agree by construction; these were written down from
    // the graph and are confronted with the oracle here, so a cell whose shape
    // was misread is caught in the fixture rather than blamed on the emitter.
    if o_od != w_od { return 10i32; }
    if o_s  != w_s  { return 11i32; }
    if o_rd != w_rd { return 12i32; }

    match __wql_tc_scc0_dred(h, de) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 13i32; }
    }
    // `cur` follows the engine only after the call returned Ok.
    let nc: Vec<E> = survivors(cur.as_slice(), de);
    cur.clear();
    let mut ci: i64 = 0i64;
    while ci < nc.len() { let e: E = nc.get(ci); cur.push(ed(e.a, e.b)); ci = ci + 1i64; }

    if h.__lat.len() != 3i64        { return 14i32; }
    if h.__lat.get(0i64) != o_od    { return 15i32; }
    if h.__lat.get(1i64) != o_s     { return 16i32; }
    // ⚠ THE SENTINEL, ASSERTED AGAINST ITSELF FIRST. `_cpt` pushes `-1` for
    // |RD|; the driver is the only party that can retire it. Checking `!= -1`
    // separately from `== o_rd` is what tells a driver that never wrote the slot
    // apart from one that wrote the wrong number — at `o_rd == 0` (cell 2) the
    // two are otherwise indistinguishable from a `-1` only by arithmetic.
    if h.__lat.get(2i64) == 0i64 - 1i64 { return 17i32; }
    if h.__lat.get(2i64) != o_rd    { return 18i32; }
    return check(h, cur.as_slice());
}

fn insert_epoch(h: &mut TcIncr, cur: &mut Vec<E>, de: &[E]) -> i32 {
    match tc_epoch(h, de) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 19i32; }
    }
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < de.len() {
        let e: E = de[i];
        if !edge_has(cur.as_slice(), e.a, e.b) { cur.push(ed(e.a, e.b)); }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return check(h, cur.as_slice());
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    // ══ CELL 1 — THE DIAMOND ═════════════════════════════════════════════
    // 1→2, 1→3, 2→4, 3→4. `T` = {12,13,24,34,14} (5 pairs). Retract (2,4):
    // OD = pairs with a walk through (2,4) = {24, 14} → |OD| = 2, |S| = 3,
    // and `closure(PRES)` = {12,13,34,14} (4 pairs) so |RD| = 1 — `(1,4)` comes
    // BACK, re-derived from the OLD survivor row `(1,3)` joined with the OLD
    // surviving edge `(3,4)`. Neither side is a delta, which is why this cell
    // fails the moment `_i`'s round 0 is narrowed to the base bodies.
    let mut h1: TcIncr = tc_incremental();
    let mut c1: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    let mut d1: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    d1.push(ed(1i64, 2i64)); d1.push(ed(1i64, 3i64));
    d1.push(ed(2i64, 4i64)); d1.push(ed(3i64, 4i64));
    let a1: i32 = insert_epoch(&mut h1, &mut c1, d1.as_slice());
    if a1 != 0i32 { return 20i32 + a1; }
    let mut r1: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    r1.push(ed(2i64, 4i64));
    let b1: i32 = retract_epoch(&mut h1, &mut c1, r1.as_slice(), 2i64, 3i64, 1i64);
    if b1 != 0i32 { return 40i32 + b1; }
    // ⚠ AND NOW INSERT AGAIN ON THE SURVIVOR HANDLE. If `__edb` had not shrunk,
    // this epoch's round-0 pass over the accumulated input re-derives `(2,4)`
    // and the retraction silently undoes itself — invisible to every check made
    // before this line.
    let mut d1b: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    d1b.push(ed(4i64, 5i64));
    let a1b: i32 = insert_epoch(&mut h1, &mut c1, d1b.as_slice());
    if a1b != 0i32 { return 60i32 + a1b; }

    // ══ CELL 2 — THE MID-CHAIN ═══════════════════════════════════════════
    // 1→2→3→4→5. `T` = 10 pairs. Retract (3,4): OD = pairs whose walk crosses
    // it = {34,35,24,25,14,15} → |OD| = 6, |S| = 4, and `closure(PRES)` = the
    // 3 pairs of 1→2→3 plus the 1 pair 4→5 = 4, so |RD| = 0. Phase 2 alone is
    // already the whole answer and phase 3 must add NOTHING.
    let mut h2: TcIncr = tc_incremental();
    let mut c2: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    let mut d2: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    d2.push(ed(1i64, 2i64)); d2.push(ed(2i64, 3i64));
    d2.push(ed(3i64, 4i64)); d2.push(ed(4i64, 5i64));
    let a2: i32 = insert_epoch(&mut h2, &mut c2, d2.as_slice());
    if a2 != 0i32 { return 80i32 + a2; }
    let mut r2: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    r2.push(ed(3i64, 4i64));
    let b2: i32 = retract_epoch(&mut h2, &mut c2, r2.as_slice(), 6i64, 4i64, 0i64);
    if b2 != 0i32 { return 100i32 + b2; }
    let mut d2b: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    d2b.push(ed(3i64, 4i64));
    // Re-inserting the very edge that was retracted: the strongest form of the
    // `__edb` witness, because the handle must arrive back at the original
    // 10-pair closure through a path that has retracted and re-derived.
    let a2b: i32 = insert_epoch(&mut h2, &mut c2, d2b.as_slice());
    if a2b != 0i32 { return 120i32 + a2b; }

    // ══ CELL 3 — THE SELF-SUPPORTING CYCLE ═══════════════════════════════
    // 1→2→3→1. `T` = all 9 ordered pairs over {1,2,3}. Retract (3,1): EVERY
    // pair has some walk through it, so |OD| = 9 and `S = ∅` — the total is
    // emptied and phase 3 re-derives `closure(PRES)` = {12,23,13} from nothing
    // but the survivor input, |RD| = 3. The cell where "the total is a LOWER
    // BOUND of the fixpoint" is the empty set.
    let mut h3: TcIncr = tc_incremental();
    let mut c3: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    let mut d3: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    d3.push(ed(1i64, 2i64)); d3.push(ed(2i64, 3i64)); d3.push(ed(3i64, 1i64));
    let a3: i32 = insert_epoch(&mut h3, &mut c3, d3.as_slice());
    if a3 != 0i32 { return 140i32 + a3; }
    let mut r3: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    r3.push(ed(3i64, 1i64));
    let b3: i32 = retract_epoch(&mut h3, &mut c3, r3.as_slice(), 9i64, 0i64, 3i64);
    if b3 != 0i32 { return 160i32 + b3; }
    let mut d3b: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    d3b.push(ed(3i64, 1i64));
    let a3b: i32 = insert_epoch(&mut h3, &mut c3, d3b.as_slice());
    if a3b != 0i32 { return 180i32 + a3b; }

    // ══ BLOCK D — `DE ⊆ EDB`, AND THE TWO SHAPES THE FOOTPRINT SENSOR MISSES
    // The handle from cell 1 is live and correct; these calls must change
    // nothing at all.
    let mut bad: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    bad.push(ed(9i64, 9i64));
    match __wql_tc_scc0_dred(&mut h1, bad.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { return 200i32; }
        Result::Err(e) => {
            match e {
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { }
                _ => { return 201i32; }
            }
        }
    }
    let d_a: i32 = check(&h1, c1.as_slice());
    if d_a != 0i32 { return 202i32; }
    // ⚠ A ROW DIFFERING IN ONE FIELD FROM AN INSERTED ONE. `(1,2)` is in the
    // input; `(1,9)` is not. On the non-rel surface a footprint that projects
    // only the fold's READ fields cannot tell these apart — an annotated-green
    // defect. `__edb` stores the exact input row, so this is refused.
    let mut bad2: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    bad2.push(ed(1i64, 9i64));
    match __wql_tc_scc0_dred(&mut h1, bad2.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { return 203i32; }
        Result::Err(_e2) => { }
    }
    let d_b: i32 = check(&h1, c1.as_slice());
    if d_b != 0i32 { return 204i32; }
    // ⚠ A MIXED BATCH: one row present, one absent. The refusal must be about
    // the BATCH, not about the first row — a driver that removed the present row
    // before discovering the absent one is exactly the non-transactional shape
    // this contract forbids, and `check` below would see `(1,2)` gone.
    let mut bad3: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    bad3.push(ed(1i64, 2i64)); bad3.push(ed(7i64, 7i64));
    match __wql_tc_scc0_dred(&mut h1, bad3.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { return 205i32; }
        Result::Err(_e3) => { }
    }
    let d_c: i32 = check(&h1, c1.as_slice());
    if d_c != 0i32 { return 206i32; }
    // ⚠⚠ THE ONE THAT MAKES THE CHECK LOAD-BEARING, AND THE THREE ABOVE ARE NOT.
    // MEASURED: with the `DE ⊆ __edb` scan deleted, `(9,9)`, `(1,9)` and the
    // mixed batch are ALL still refused — by `_cpt`'s `OD ⊄ T` pass, one phase
    // later. Their `Err` says nothing about this check, exactly the way a
    // name-absence door says nothing about which refusal produced it.
    // THIS ROW IS DIFFERENT. `h1`'s input is {12, 13, 34, 45}; the pair `(1,4)`
    // is in the RELATION (derived, 1→3→4) and is NOT an edge. So `_od`'s base
    // body over DE derives `path(1,4)`, which IS in T, `_odp` extends it to
    // `(1,5)` which is also in T, and `OD ⊆ T` HOLDS — `_cpt` sees nothing wrong
    // and the whole retraction runs to `Ok`, over-deleting and re-deriving two
    // facts that no caller ever inserted. The DE ⊆ EDB check is the only thing
    // in the system that can tell an INPUT row from a DERIVED one, which is what
    // makes it a check about the SURFACE rather than a second opinion on `_cpt`.
    // CONTROL, MEASURED: delete the check and this call returns `Ok` — exit 207.
    let mut bad4: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    bad4.push(ed(1i64, 4i64));
    match __wql_tc_scc0_dred(&mut h1, bad4.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { return 207i32; }
        Result::Err(_e4) => { }
    }
    let d_d: i32 = check(&h1, c1.as_slice());
    if d_d != 0i32 { return 208i32; }

    // ══ BLOCK E — THE ERROR CONTRACT, PINNED BY A **LATE** FAULT ═════════
    // `sq` sums the second column. Edges (1,2) and (2,MIN) give the relation
    // {(1,2), (2,MIN), (1,MIN)}, so group 1 sums to `2 + MIN` and group 2 to
    // `MIN` — all representable, and the INSERT path never multiplies.
    // Retracting (2,MIN) over-deletes {(2,MIN), (1,MIN)}; the driver then folds
    // group 1's removed row at −1, computes `el_mul(-1, MIN)`, and OVERFLOWS —
    // AFTER `_od`, `_odp`, `_cpt` and `_i` have all run and after the survivor
    // input, both totals and the latch have been written to its private copy.
    let mn: i64 = 0i64 - 9223372036854775807i64 - 1i64;
    let mut hs: SqIncr = sq_incremental();
    let mut ds: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    ds.push(ed(1i64, 2i64)); ds.push(ed(2i64, mn));
    match sq_epoch(&mut hs, ds.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 210i32; }
    }
    // THE PRE-CALL STATE, recorded so "byte-identical" is a comparison and not
    // an adjective.
    let pre_snap: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match sq_snapshot(&hs) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 211i32; }
    };
    let cl_pre: Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_closure(ds.as_slice());
    let want_pre: Vec<(i64, i64)> = sums_of(&cl_pre);
    if !same_set(&pre_snap, &want_pre) { return 212i32; }
    let pre_edb: i64 = hs.__edb.len();
    let pre_tot: i64 = hs.__tot_spath.len();
    let pre_lat: i64 = hs.__lat.len();
    // ⚠ AN EXACT ROW SEQUENCE, NOT A SET. `_cpt` compacts IN PLACE and preserves
    // the survivors' relative order, and `_i` APPENDS — so a driver that had
    // committed a compacted-and-re-derived total would come back with the same
    // ROWS in a different ORDER on some shapes. Order is the property that
    // distinguishes "never touched" from "touched and restored".
    let mut pre_seq: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut pi: i64 = 0i64;
    while pi < hs.__tot_spath.len() {
        let p: (i64, i64) = hs.__tot_spath.get(pi);
        pre_seq.push((p.0, p.1));
        pi = pi + 1i64;
    }

    let mut rs: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    rs.push(ed(2i64, mn));
    match __wql_sq_scc0_dred(&mut hs, rs.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { return 213i32; }
        Result::Err(_e) => { }
    }
    // THE CONTRACT: byte-identical, content AND order, in every field.
    if hs.__edb.len()       != pre_edb { return 214i32; }
    if hs.__tot_spath.len() != pre_tot { return 215i32; }
    if hs.__lat.len()       != pre_lat { return 216i32; }
    let mut qi: i64 = 0i64;
    while qi < pre_seq.len() {
        let want_p: (i64, i64) = pre_seq.get(qi);
        let got_p:  (i64, i64) = hs.__tot_spath.get(qi);
        if got_p.0 != want_p.0 || got_p.1 != want_p.1 { return 217i32; }
        qi = qi + 1i64;
    }
    let post_snap: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match sq_snapshot(&hs) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 218i32; }
    };
    if !same_set(&post_snap, &want_pre) { return 219i32; }
    // AND THE HANDLE IS STILL LIVE: a faulted retraction is not a poisoned
    // handle, so the next epoch must work. This is the half the interpreter's
    // ABSORBING faulted state fails.
    let mut ds2: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    ds2.push(ed(2i64, 3i64));
    match sq_epoch(&mut hs, ds2.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 220i32; }
    }
    let mut all2: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    all2.push(ed(1i64, 2i64)); all2.push(ed(2i64, mn)); all2.push(ed(2i64, 3i64));
    let live_snap: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match sq_snapshot(&hs) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 221i32; }
    };
    let cl_live: Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_closure(all2.as_slice());
    let want_live: Vec<(i64, i64)> = sums_of(&cl_live);
    if !same_set(&live_snap, &want_live) { return 222i32; }

    return 0i32;
}
